airs and turn your computer on and  get logged in and look it up but now you  do there's just no opportunity you  instantly look it up on your phone right  away when you're driving I don't get  lost anymore right I mean yes in Boston  sometimes even with GPS you end up in a  tunnel as opposed to on a bridge and  then you're out of town but the GPS  would know right away exactly how to get  back I used to have to sometimes think  about okay  I have my printed directions to go from  A to B but I ended up at spot C so I've  got to get back on my path maybe that is  something that is good for human brains  to be lost sometime or whatever what's  your general reaction to some of these  arguments that are made well we probably  are losing something yes by having all  of this information readily available at  our fingertips but imagine where we  would be without any of it right so I'm  for it I don't think we're better off  without it and perhaps there will be a  tipping point where where we realize  things have gone too far and and there  might be some type of movement to kind  of bring us back down I mean let's to go  through the first issue that I mentioned  the idea that we're now craving constant  stimulation and whatever yeah it's true  that sort of scrolling on your  smartphone does release dopamine in a  way that's reminiscent of using other  substances or other activities in that  sense however sometimes you see these  pictures of oh everybody on the subways  on their smartphones but then you see  pictures from the 60s where everybody  has their head down in a newspaper so is  that really different because it's a  smartphone instead of a newspaper I  don't know I guess you sort of have to  be the judge of that some people just  can't sit still and observe what's  happening around them right they just  happen to be looking at the cell phone  so it's it you might find it annoying  you might not enjoy being in the company  of these people but if it wasn't the  cell phone like you said it would be a  newspaper it would be something else or  they yeah does that make sense it does  and as far as the you know less  opportunities for really thinking about  things I would make a counter argument  which is when you're sitting around with  people and the topic comes up like for  example does gravity bend light yes  you're not spending as much time  thinking about whether gravity bends  light you're instantly able to look that  up on your phone but maybe that allows  you more time to actually think about  concepts where you can't look up the  answer right you can get past the facts  and  move on to the ideas more quickly I  don't know I mean I'm just sort of  thinking out loud here when you think  about how what is the impact on society  biddin of the fact that now we have  instantaneous communication yeah you  don't need to spend time debating or  wondering when was the telephone  developed and in what year was TV  developed right you have those facts  available to you instantly but maybe you  get past the facts and then you can  really get into talking about how these  technologies have affected you or  someone else so I don't I don't know I  mean yes you don't spend as much time  just trying to remember fact that's true  but you could make the argument that  that opens up the door to more time  spent on other types of thought true but  here we are and we have the cell phones  everybody has a smartphone and it makes  life much easier for us and at this  point if you were just to take the  smartphones away people would find  something new to waste time on well that  but we would we would find ourselves at  a disadvantage so it would have to be  for us to just get rid of them or find  that we don't need to use them there  would have to be a fundamental shift of  things and there is sort of a pragmatic  reality here which is if you just decide  for yourself you are no longer going to  have a smartphone at all and everybody  else still do